4. React Implementation of One Feature
Selected Feature: Product Search & Filtering
The Product Search & Filtering feature will be implemented using multiple reusable React components and state variables. The main components will be SearchBar, FilterPanel, ProductList, and ProductCard. The ProductList component can manage the main search and filter state, while SearchBar and FilterPanel will receive state values and callback functions through props. ProductList will filter the available products and pass the resulting product data to ProductCard components.
Example React state:
const [searchText, setSearchText] = useState("");
const [category, setCategory] = useState("All");
const [priceRange, setPriceRange] = useState("All");
const [products, setProducts] = useState([]);
Components and shared data:
•	SearchBar: Shares searchText and the setSearchText callback with the parent component.
•	FilterPanel: Shares selected category and priceRange values with the parent component.
•	ProductList: Maintains or receives the filtering state and produces the filtered product array.
•	ProductCard: Receives individual product information such as id, name, image, price, rating, and category through props.
Data Flow:
SearchBar → searchText → ProductList
FilterPanel → category / priceRange → ProductList
ProductList → filteredProducts → ProductCard
ProductCard → selected product → Product Details page
Whenever the user changes the search text or filters, React updates the corresponding state. The ProductList component then filters the product data and renders only the matching ProductCard components. This approach keeps the UI modular, reusable, and easy to maintain.
